PythonQt.Qt.QDirIterator.doc:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

You can use QDirIterator to navigate entries of a directory one at a time. It is similar
to [QDir::entryList](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdir.html#entryList)() and
[QDir::entryInfoList](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdir.html#entryInfoList)(), but
because it lists entries one at a time instead of all at once, it scales better and is
more suitable for large directories. It also supports listing directory contents
recursively, and following symbolic links. Unlike
[QDir::entryList](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdir.html#entryList)(),
QDirIterator does not support sorting.

The QDirIterator constructor takes a
[QDir](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdir.html) or a directory as argument. After
construction, the iterator is located before the first directory entry. Here's how to
iterate over all the entries sequentially:

```
QDirIterator it("/etc", QDirIterator::Subdirectories);
while (it.hasNext()) {
    QString dir = it.next();
    qDebug() << dir;
    // /etc/.
    // /etc/..
    // /etc/X11
    // /etc/X11/fs
    // ...
}
```

Here's how to find and read all files filtered by name, recursively:

```
QDirIterator it("/sys", QStringList() << "scaling_cur_freq", QDir::NoFilter, QDirIterator::Subdirectories);
while (it.hasNext()) {
    QFile f(it.next());
    f.open(QIODevice::ReadOnly);
    qDebug() << f.fileName() << f.readAll().trimmed().toDouble() / 1000 << "MHz";
}
```

The [next](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdiriterator.html#next)() function returns
the path to the next directory entry and advances the iterator. You can also call
[filePath](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdiriterator.html#filePath)() to get the
current file path without advancing the iterator. The
[fileName](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdiriterator.html#fileName)() function
returns only the name of the file, similar to how
[QDir::entryList](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdir.html#entryList)() works. You
can also call
[fileInfo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdiriterator.html#fileInfo)() to get a
[QFileInfo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qfileinfo.html) for the current entry.

Unlike Qt's container iterators, QDirIterator is uni-directional (i.e., you cannot
iterate directories in reverse order) and does not allow random access.

PythonQt.Qt.QDnsLookup.doc:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

QDnsLookup uses the mechanisms provided by the operating system to perform DNS lookups.
To perform a lookup you need to specify a
[name](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdnslookup.html#name-prop) and
[type](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdnslookup.html#type-prop) then invoke the
[lookup()](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdnslookup.html#lookup) slot. The
[finished()](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdnslookup.html#finished) signal will be
emitted upon completion.

For example, you can determine which servers an XMPP chat client should connect to for a
given domain with:

```
void MyObject::lookupServers()
{
    // Create a DNS lookup.
    dns = new QDnsLookup(this);
    connect(dns, SIGNAL(finished()),
            this, SLOT(handleServers()));

    // Find the XMPP servers for gmail.com
    dns->setType(QDnsLookup::SRV);
    dns->setName("_xmpp-client._tcp.gmail.com");
    dns->lookup();
}
```

Once the request finishes you can handle the results with:

```
void MyObject::handleServers()
{
    // Check the lookup succeeded.
    if (dns->error() != QDnsLookup::NoError) {
        qWarning("DNS lookup failed");
        dns->deleteLater();
        return;
    }

    // Handle the results.
    const auto records = dns->serviceRecords();
    for (const QDnsServiceRecord &record : records) {
        ...
    }
    dns->deleteLater();
}
```

**Note:** If you simply want to find the IP address(es) associated with a host name, or
the host name associated with an IP address you should use
[QHostInfo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qhostinfo.html) instead.

PythonQt.Qt.QEventTransition.doc:
Qt 5 reference:

## Detailed Description

A QEventTransition object binds an event to a particular
[QObject](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qobject.html). QEventTransition is part of
[The State Machine Framework](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/statemachine-api.html).

Example:

```
QPushButton *button = ...;
QState *s1 = ...;
QState *s2 = ...;
// If in s1 and the button receives an Enter event, transition to s2
QEventTransition *enterTransition = new QEventTransition(button, QEvent::Enter);
enterTransition->setTargetState(s2);
s1->addTransition(enterTransition);
// If in s2 and the button receives an Exit event, transition back to s1
QEventTransition *leaveTransition = new QEventTransition(button, QEvent::Leave);
leaveTransition->setTargetState(s1);
s2->addTransition(leaveTransition);
```

### Subclassing

When reimplementing the
[eventTest](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qeventtransition.html#eventTest)()
function, you should first call the base implementation to verify that the event is a
[QStateMachine::WrappedEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstatemachine-wrappedevent.html)
for the proper object and event type. You may then cast the event to a
[QStateMachine::WrappedEvent](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstatemachine-wrappedevent.html)
and get the original event by calling
[QStateMachine::WrappedEvent::event](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstatemachine-wrappedevent.html#event)(),
and perform additional checks on that object.

